<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<pretext xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<!-- Worksheet contributed by Courtney Gibbons -->
 
<article xml:id="wkst-apportionment">

<title>Apportionment: Hamilton, Webster, Huntington-Hill, and Dean get in line!</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this activity you will treat apportionment methods as functions from a continuous
      <em>quota space</em> to an <em>apportionment space</em> (sometimes called an <em>allocation space</em>), 
      <me>F: \mathcal{Q} = \{(a,b) \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^2 \, \colon \, a+b = M\} to \mathcal{M} = \{(k,\ell) \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^2 \, \colon \, k+\ell = M\}</me> to discrete integer allocations. You will visualize apportionment methods as geometric partitions of the line segment <m>\mathcal{Q}</m> that determine how to apportion the seats. That is, you're going to find <m>F^{-1}(\mathbf{m})</m> for each <m> \mathbf{m} \in \mathcal{M}</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Warm-Up: Two Groups, Five Seats</title>
    <p>
      Before working in triangles and three dimensions, we begin with the simplest possible
      case: two groups sharing a fixed number of seats.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose two groups, A and B, are dividing 5 seats.
      If group A receives <m>a</m> seats, then group B receives <m>5-a</m>.
      So every possible allocation corresponds to a point on the line segment
    </p>

    <me>
      0 \le a \le 5.
    </me>

    <p>
      In this activity, you will compare Hamilton’s method and Webster’s method
      in this one-dimensional setting.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ===================================================== -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="quota-line">
    <title>Quota Space on a Line</title>

    <p>
      Think of <m>a</m> as the (possibly fractional) quota for group A.
      The quota for group B is automatically <m>5-a</m>.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="integer-points">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Mark the integer points on the number line from 0 to 5.
        </p>

        <p>
          What do these integer points represent in terms of seat allocations?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
          <p>
              The integer points are <m>0,1,2,3,4,5</m>.
              The point <m>k</m> represents the allocation <q>A gets <m>k</m> seats and B gets <m>5-k</m> seats,</q>
              i.e. <m>(k,5-k)</m>.
          </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fractional-meaning">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does an integer like <m>a=2.7</m> (or the ordered pair <m>(2.7,2.3)</m>) represent in this setting?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
            <p>
              It represents a quota (ideal fractional allocation) where A <q>deserves</q> about <m>2.7</m> seats and B about
              <m>5-2.7=2.3</m> seats. An apportionment method will turn this into an integer allocation near
              <m>(3,2)</m> or <m>(2,3)</m>. Which one depends on the method.
            </p>
          </solution>
    </exercise>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="hamilton-line">
    <title>Hamilton’s Method on the Line</title>

    <p>
      Hamilton’s method works by first taking lower quotas and then assigning
      leftover seats based on the size of the fractional parts.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the two-group case, this process simplifies dramatically!
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="hamilton-floor">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose group A has quota <m>a</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          (a) Write the lower quota for group A.
        </p>

        <p>
          (b) Write the lower quota for group B.
        </p>

        <p>
          (c) How many seats remain to be assigned after taking floors?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
            <p>
              (a) A’s lower quota is <m>\lfloor a\rfloor</m>.
              (b) B’s lower quota is <m>\lfloor 5-a\rfloor</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              (c) The remaining seats are
              <m>5-(\lfloor a\rfloor+\lfloor 5-a\rfloor)</m>.
              For non-integer <m>a</m>, we have <m>\lfloor a\rfloor+\lfloor 5-a\rfloor=4</m>, so there is 1
              leftover seat. (If <m>a</m> is an integer, there are no leftover seats.)
            </p>
          </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="largest-remainder-line">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that Hamilton’s method gives the extra seat to group A exactly when
        </p>

        <me>
          a - \lfloor a \rfloor \ge (5-a) - \lfloor 5-a \rfloor.
        </me>

        <p>
          Simplify this inequality.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
            <p>
              Write the remainders:
              <m>r_A=a-\lfloor a\rfloor</m> and <m>r_B=(5-a)-\lfloor 5-a\rfloor</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              For non-integer <m>a</m>, <m>\lceil a\rceil=\lfloor a\rfloor+1</m> and
              <m>\lfloor 5-a\rfloor=5-\lceil a\rceil</m>, so
              <m>r_B=(5-a)-(5-\lceil a\rceil)=\lceil a\rceil-a=\lfloor a\rfloor+1-a</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              Then <m>r_A \ge r_B</m> becomes
              <me>
                a-\lfloor a\rfloor \ge \lfloor a\rfloor+1-a
                \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
                2a \ge 2\lfloor a\rfloor+1
                \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
                a \ge \lfloor a\rfloor+\tfrac12.
              </me>
              So, within each unit interval <m>[k,k+1)</m>, the <q>switch point</q> is <m>k+\tfrac12</m>.
            </p>
          </solution> -->
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="hamilton-threshold">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>a</m> where the seat allocation switches.
        </p>

        <p>
          Draw this point on the number line and label the intervals where Hamilton assigns:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>2 seats to A,</li>
          <li>3 seats to A.</li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
            <p>
              The switch between A getting 2 vs 3 seats occurs on the interval <m>[2,3)</m>, at
              <m>a=2.5</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              For <m>2 \le a \lt 2.5</m>, Hamilton gives A 2 seats (and B 3).
              For <m>2.5 \le a \lt 3</m>, Hamilton gives A 3 seats (and B 2).
              (At exactly <m>a=2.5</m> there is a tie in remainders.)
            </p>
          </solution> -->
    </exercise>

  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ===================================================== -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="webster-line">
    <title>Webster’s Method on the Line</title>

    <p>
      Webster’s method uses a scaling factor (the divisor) and then rounds to the nearest integer.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the two-group case, Webster’s behavior becomes especially simple.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="rounding-rule-line">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose Webster uses the quota <m>a</m> directly.
          Write the condition under which group A is rounded to 3 seats.
        </p>

        <p>
          Express your answer as an inequality involving <m>a</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="webster-threshold">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>a</m> where Webster switches from giving A 2 seats to giving A 3 seats.
        </p>

        <p>
          Mark this point on the same number line you used for Hamilton.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="hill-line">
  <title>Huntington–Hill (Equal Proportions) on the Line</title>

  <p>
    Now we add one more method, often called <em>Huntington–Hill</em> or
    <em>Equal Proportions</em>.
    In this warm-up we will describe it as a next-seat rule.
  </p>

  <p>
    We will use the common convention that each group must receive at least 1 seat.
    So with 5 total seats and two groups, the possible final allocations are
    <m>(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)</m>.
  </p>
</paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="hill-priority">
    <title>Step 1: A next-seat score</title>

    <p>
      Suppose a group has population <m>p</m> and currently has <m>n</m> seats.
      Its score for receiving the next seat is
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathrm{score}(p,n)=\frac{p}{\sqrt{n(n+1)}}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The next seat goes to the group with the larger score.
      (If there is a tie, use a tie-breaking rule.)
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="hill-scores-table">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Starting from <m>(1,1)</m>, there are 3 more seats to give out.
          Make a small table of the scores that would be compared at each step:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>When comparing A goes from 1 to 2 versus B goes from 1 to 2.</li>
          <li>When comparing A goes from 2 to 3 versus B goes from 1 to 2.</li>
          <li>When comparing A goes from 3 to 4 versus B goes from 1 to 2.</li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The score for going from <m>n</m> to <m>n+1</m> is
          <m>p/\sqrt{n(n+1)}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Comparing A: <m>1 \to 2</m> versus B: <m>1 \to 2</m> compares
          <m>\dfrac{p_A}{\sqrt{1\cdot 2}}</m> to <m>\dfrac{p_B}{\sqrt{1\cdot 2}}</m>,
          so the first extra seat goes to whichever population is larger.
        </p>

        <p>
          Later comparisons involve different denominators:
          A: <m>2\to 3</m> uses <m>\sqrt{2\cdot 3}=\sqrt{6}</m>, while B: <m>1\to 2</m> uses <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, etc.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="hill-cutpoints">
    <title>Step 2: Where does the outcome switch?</title>

    <p>
      As before, let <m>a</m> be A’s quota on the line, so B’s quota is <m>5-a</m>.
      The Huntington–Hill method partitions the line into intervals where A receives
      1, 2, 3, or 4 seats.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="hill-thresholds-derivation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The middle switch between A getting 2 seats versus 3 seats happens at <m>a=2.5</m>.
          (This is the symmetric point where A and B have equal population.)
        </p>

        <p>
          The other two switch points occur when the population ratio reaches <m>\sqrt{6}</m>
          (or its reciprocal).
          Use the score comparison to show that the boundary between outcomes
          <m>(1,4)</m> and <m>(2,3)</m> occurs when
        </p>

        <me>
          \frac{p_A}{\sqrt{2}}=\frac{p_B}{\sqrt{6}}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Then solve for the corresponding quota value <m>a</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          The boundary between final outcomes <m>(1,4)</m> and <m>(2,3)</m> is the point where,
          after A already has 1 seat, A is tied with B for receiving the next relevant seat.
          One clean way to capture the decisive tie is:
          compare A’s score for <m>1\to 2</m> with B’s score for <m>2\to 3</m> (which uses <m>\sqrt{6}</m>).
          Setting them equal gives
          <m>\dfrac{p_A}{\sqrt{2}}=\dfrac{p_B}{\sqrt{6}}</m>, i.e.
          <m>\dfrac{p_A}{p_B}=\sqrt{\dfrac{2}{6}}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}</m>.
          (Equivalently, a symmetric formulation yields the same quota cut point.)
        </p>

        <p>
          A more standard (and symmetric) way to express the decisive cut points for 5 seats is:
          A receives 1 seat when <m>\dfrac{p_A}{p_B} \lt \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}</m>,
          and A receives 4 seats when <m>\dfrac{p_A}{p_B} \gt \sqrt{6}</m>.
          Converting to quota on the line:
          <m>a=5\cdot \dfrac{p_A}{p_A+p_B}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          If <m>\dfrac{p_A}{p_B}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}</m>, then
          <m>\dfrac{p_A}{p_A+p_B}=\dfrac{1/\sqrt{6}}{1+1/\sqrt{6}}=\dfrac{1}{1+\sqrt{6}}</m>,
          so
          <me>a=\frac{5}{1+\sqrt{6}}\approx 1.4494897.</me>
        </p>

        <p>
          By symmetry, the boundary between 3 and 4 seats occurs at
          <me>a=\frac{5\sqrt{6}}{1+\sqrt{6}}\approx 3.5505103.</me>
        </p>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="hill-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the switch points to label the intervals on the line where A receives
          1, 2, 3, or 4 seats under Huntington–Hill.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          Let
          <m>t_a=\dfrac{5}{1+\sqrt{6}}\approx 1.4494897</m>
          and
          <m>t_b=\dfrac{5\sqrt{6}}{1+\sqrt{6}}\approx 3.5505103</m>.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><m>0 \le a \lt t_a</m>: A gets 1 seat (allocation <m>(1,4)</m>).</li>
          <li><m>a \le a \lt 2.5</m>: A gets 2 seats (allocation <m>(2,3)</m>).</li>
          <li><m>2.5 \le a \lt t_b</m>: A gets 3 seats (allocation <m>(3,2)</m>).</li>
          <li><m>b \le a \le 5</m>: A gets 4 seats (allocation <m>(4,1)</m>).</li>
        </ul>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>
  </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="dean-line">
  <title>Dean’s Method on the Line</title>

  <p>
    Dean’s method is another divisor method, similar in structure to Webster’s and Huntington–Hill’s.
    The difference lies in how rounding is decided.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead of rounding to the nearest integer or using geometric means,
    Dean’s method uses the <em>harmonic mean</em> as the cutoff between adjacent integers.
  </p>
</paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="dean-rounding">
    <title>Step 1: Dean’s Rounding Rule</title>

    <p>
      Suppose a quota value <m>q</m> lies between two integers <m>n</m> and <m>n+1</m>.
      Dean’s method rounds:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>down to <m>n</m> if <m>q</m> is less than the harmonic mean of <m>n</m> and <m>n+1</m>,</li>
      <li>up to <m>n+1</m> if <m>q</m> is greater than that harmonic mean.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Recall that the harmonic mean of <m>n</m> and <m>n+1</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      H(n,n+1)=\frac{2n(n+1)}{2n+1}.
    </me>

    <exercise xml:id="dean-cutoff-example">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute the harmonic mean cutoff between:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>1 and 2</li>
          <li>2 and 3</li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          Between 1 and 2:
          <me>
            H(1,2)=\frac{2\cdot1\cdot2}{2\cdot1+1}=\frac{4}{3}\approx1.3333.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Between 2 and 3:
          <me>
            H(2,3)=\frac{2\cdot2\cdot3}{2\cdot2+1}=\frac{12}{5}=2.4.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="dean-line-intervals">
    <title>Step 2: Dean’s Switching Points on the Line</title>

    <p>
      As before, let <m>a</m> represent A’s quota on the line with total 5 seats.
      Dean’s rounding thresholds determine when A switches from receiving
      2 seats to 3 seats, and from 1 to 2, etc.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="dean-middle-switch">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the harmonic mean cutoff between 2 and 3, determine the value of <m>a</m>
          where Dean switches from assigning A 2 seats to assigning A 3 seats.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          The cutoff between 2 and 3 is <m>2.4</m>.
          So Dean rounds to 3 seats when <m>a \ge 2.4</m>, and to 2 seats when <m>a \lt 2.4</m>.
        </p>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="dean-other-switches">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the harmonic mean cutoffs to find the approximate switch points where A moves:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>from 1 seat to 2 seats,</li>
          <li>from 3 seats to 4 seats.</li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Between 1 and 2 seats, the cutoff is <m>4/3 \approx 1.333</m>.
          So A switches from 1 to 2 seats at <m>a \approx 1.333</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          By symmetry, the cutoff between 3 and 4 seats occurs at
          <m>5-4/3=3.6667</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="dean-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Label the intervals on the number line where Dean assigns A:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>1 seat</li>
          <li>2 seats</li>
          <li>3 seats</li>
          <li>4 seats</li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          Approximately:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><m>0 \le a \lt 1.333</m>: A gets 1 seat</li>
          <li><m>1.333 \le a \lt 2.4</m>: A gets 2 seats</li>
          <li><m>2.4 \le a \lt 3.667</m>: A gets 3 seats</li>
          <li><m>3.667 \le a \le 5</m>: A gets 4 seats</li>
        </ul>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="dean-compare">
    <title>Comparing All Four Methods</title>

    <exercise xml:id="compare-all">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare the switching points for Hamilton, Webster, Huntington–Hill, and Dean
          for the transition between 2 seats and 3 seats.
        </p>

        <p>
          Arrange the methods from earliest switch to latest switch as <m>a</m> increases.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          Switching points for A going from 2 to 3 seats:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Dean: <m>a=2.4</m></li>
          <li>Hamilton: <m>a=2.5</m></li>
          <li>Webster: <m>a=2.5</m></li>
          <li>Huntington–Hill: <m>a=2.5</m></li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          So Dean switches earliest, while the other three coincide at the midpoint for this case.
        </p>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="interpretation-dean">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In words: what does Dean’s method favor compared to Webster and Huntington–Hill?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <!-- <solution>
        <p>
          Dean’s method favors giving additional seats slightly earlier (at smaller quota values).
          Geometrically, its rounding boundaries are shifted toward the lower integer compared to
          arithmetic and geometric mean rounding.
        </p>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>
</paragraphs>

</article>
